# Content Voice (US English)

## Punctuation

- Do not use em dashes (`—`) in user-facing copy.
- Use a period, comma, colon, or parentheses instead.
- SEO/OG titles: use pipe (`|`) between segments.
- Label pairs (e.g. case study industry + title): use a colon.

## Voice

- Write like Robert Terry: direct, specific, first person on marketing pages.
- Use natural US English contractions in body copy (`you're`, `I'll`, `don't`).
- Use the Oxford comma in lists.
- Prefer short sentences and concrete examples (hours saved, user counts, file types).
- Avoid corporate or AI phrasing.

## Banned or replace

| Avoid | Use instead |
|-------|-------------|
| Comprehensive training | Team walkthrough and written guides |
| Deep-dive audit | Detailed review of your files and workflow |
| friction in your data processes | manual steps that slow your team down |
| you are in the right place | If the file crashes and work stops, I can usually fix it. |
| robust / streamlined / seamless | stable / faster / works without extra steps |
| leverage (verb) | use |
| no obligation — just | no pressure. Just |
| hands-on (overused) | direct / working with your files |
| Expert advice / Expert tips | Practical notes / from X years of client work |
| Wondering how much… | What … actually costs… |
| Learn the / Learn how to (meta copy) | Direct statement: What causes… / How to… |
| No obligation | No pressure |
| Operational friction | manual work / slow processes |
| transform / unlock | fix / improve / automate |
| innovative / cutting-edge | proven / practical / tested |
| future-ready / next-generation | reliable / maintainable / scalable |
| powerful solutions | fixed-price work / stable systems |
| game-changing / revolutionize | cut hours / reduce crashes / migrate safely |
| seamless | works without extra steps / stable |
| leverage synergies | combine / use together |

## Preferred verbs

fix, repair, automate, migrate, improve, support, reduce, simplify, maintain, optimize, stabilize, modernize

## SEO

- Keep primary keywords in H1, first paragraph, and 2+ H2s on service and blog pages.
- Meta descriptions: plain US English, no em dashes, under 155 characters when possible.
